## Who logs in and what they can do

There are three roles on a **membership** (user ↔ company):

| Role | Can |
|------|-----|
| **Owner** | Everything, including inviting people. **The only role that sees pay.** |
| **Manager** | Everything except invites and pay. Managers approve or reopen days, edit the Planning board, set material norms and stock levels, and send or reject material requests. They also set up schedules, holidays and pay groups, enter hours in the timesheet, approve days off, and approve each pay group's hours. |
| **Team lead** | Read projects their teams are on; fill reports and sheet cells for their teams; record crew attendance (with hours), issues, notes and photos on those days; record material used on their sites; request material; move/comment on tasks assigned to their teams. They can ask for days off for their own team and read their team's timesheet. |

Pay is behind its own permission, `CanSeePay` in `companies/permissions.py`, which allows the owner only. It never appears in shared serializers or the activity feed. Invites can only create managers and leads, so each company has one owner.

The active company lives in the **session** (`company_id`). Almost every API call does:

1. Require login
2. Require a membership for that session company
3. Filter querysets to that company
4. If the user is a team lead, narrow further (`scope_for_lead`) and only allow extra writes listed on the view as `lead_actions`

That is the tenancy wall. Two companies never share rows; PKs can coincide, so activity lookups also filter by company. Every Phase 1 model has tests proving a second company cannot read or write it.

Demo login (from seed): `manager@example.com` / `Manager-pass-123` on **River Co**. The seed also has a team lead (Mira) and demo data for every Phase 1 screen.

