## Domain model (the important tables)

**Identity & tenancy** (`accounts`, `companies`)
User → Membership(role) → Company. Session stores which company is active. `Company.timezone` (default `UTC`) and `company.today()` decide what "today" means.

**People** (`workforce`)
`Team` (number, worker count, lead note, optional `lead_user`). `Worker` with type, optional team, `hired_on` / `left_on` and an optional yearly `leave_allowance`. Crew on a report is always a **team**, not an individual. Employment dates, not `status`, decide who can have hours on a day, so a past crew that includes someone who has left can still be corrected.
`TeamMembership` (worker, team, start_date, end_date) is the history. At most one open stretch per worker (database constraint). `move_worker(worker, team, on_date)` closes the old stretch the day before and opens a new one. `Worker.team` stays as the current team, so older screens and APIs keep working. A team's size shows active members, with `worker_count` as the fallback for teams with no registered workers.

**Catalogs** (`operations` + `work`)
Same pattern everywhere: company-scoped name, `position`, unique `(company, name)`, `PROTECT` on FKs so you cannot delete a measure still used by a work type.

- Work kind → measure (m²) → work type (category + measure)
- Duration unit (`days_per_unit`, optional timeline step)
- Task category → status; task phase

Creating a company seeds defaults (To do / In progress / …, Day/Week/Month, Area/Volume, etc.).

**Projects** (`operations.Workplace`)
Still named “workplace” in the database; the UI says **project**. Routes exist as both `/api/projects/` and `/api/workplaces/`. `WorkplaceTeam` placements have `start_date` / `end_date`; `active_on(date)` (in `workforce/services.py`) is the one helper for "covers this date", used for placements and memberships alike.

**Progress**
`ProjectScope` holds planned quantity. Done is a **subquery Sum** of matching `SiteReport`s (not a join Sum, so rows do not explode). `scopes_with_progress()` annotates it on a whole queryset, so project lists no longer run one query per scope. Remaining is planned − done. Pace and forecasts live in `operations/progress.py` (see below).

**Daily log** (`sitelog`, new app)
- `DailyLog` — one per project + date: status `open` / `approved`, weather, notes, approved_by/at. Created on first write to that day.
- `DailyLogIssue` — kind, description, hours lost, resolved.
- `CrewAttendance` — worker + project + date + team + **hours**; unique per worker per project day.
  - The team must be placed on the project that day.
  - Newly added workers must be employed that day.
  - A worker can be on only one team's crew at that project that day, at most 24 h across projects and other work, and never in the future.
  - Worker and team FKs are `RESTRICT`, so deleting a worker or team with recorded crew is refused, and deleting a project with crew hours is refused in its view.
- `set_attendance` is the one write path for crew. The Daily log and the timesheet (`set_worker_hours`, `fill_from_schedule`) all go through it.
- `ensure_day_open(workplace, date)` is the project-day lock. Every write path for a project day calls it.

**Time** (`timesheets`)
- `WorkSchedule` + `WorkScheduleVersion`: hours per weekday from a date on, plus `day_hours` (a full day).
  - Changing hours adds a version, so earlier days keep their hours.
  - `ScheduleAssignment` puts a worker (or, with no worker, everyone) on a schedule for a stretch of time.
- `Holiday`: company calendar.
- `LeaveType` (catalog with `paid` and `uses_allowance`, which are fixed once used) and `Leave` (a day off from–to, half days, requested / approved / rejected / cancelled).
- `OtherWork`: hours away from any project (yard, office, travel, training).
- `PayGroup` (cycle), `PayGroupAssignment` (who is in which group, dated) and `TimesheetPeriod` (a group's cycle, approved or open).
- `schedule.py` (`WorkCalendar`): scheduled hours (raw schedule), expected hours (minus holidays and days outside employment) and norm (a full day).
- `sheet.py` (`build_timesheet`): every worker × day, from about ten bulk queries.
- `locks.py`: `ensure_worker_open` / `ensure_company_open`, the period lock. Writes that can change past hours, days off or expected hours call it.

**Pay** (`payroll`, owner only)
- `PaySettings`: currency, extra-hours multiplier (empty means not paid), and whether day-rate workers are paid for holidays.
- `PayRate`: day, week, month or "not paid here", from a start date.
- `PayAdjustment`: a bonus or deduction. It is attached to the next pay approved after its date.
- `Payment`: an advance or wages, on any day.
- `PayRun` (one per approved period) and `PayLine` (the frozen amounts).
- Owed = approved nets − payments.

**Work** (`work.Task`)
Task belongs to company + project; optional team, work type, phase; status required. Comments and `ActivityEvent` (`core`) record created/moved/commented, and now day approved/reopened.

**Attachments** (`core.Attachment`)
Same generic `target_type` / `target_id` pattern as `ActivityEvent`, plus file, original name, content type, size, caption, uploader. Allowed: JPEG, PNG, WebP, PDF up to 15 MB; the file's first bytes must match its type. Files are stored as `attachments/company_<id>/<random>.<ext>` and downloaded only through `/api/attachments/{id}/file/`, which checks the company. There is no public `/media/` URL.

**Sheet**
`SheetColumn`: kind `work_type` | `team` | `text`.
`SheetTextCell`: free text per column + date (e.g. weather).
Quantity cells are not a third table — they **are** site reports.

**Inventory**
`Location` (DEPOT or SITE), lots, append-only movements. Receive only into depot. New in Phase 1:

- `StockMovement.scope` — a consume can name the work type it was used for (only consumes, only that project's scopes).
- `WorkTypeMaterial` — the material norm: quantity of an item per unit of a work type. Edited on the Work types page.
- `StockLevel` — minimum quantity of an item at a location; below it, the item is on the "Running low" list.
- `MaterialRequest` + `MaterialRequestLine` — status `open` / `partly_fulfilled` / `fulfilled` / `rejected`. Fulfilling creates TRANSFER movements through the normal `apply_movement`, tagged with the request.

